manual.description:
{image}

Enter the IEEE address of the remote, for example 0x1035970000a4c168. The friendly name from Zigbee2MQTT works as well, as long as it matches the topic exactly.

The address must be the last part of the device topic - the part after the base topic.

mode_actions.description:
{image_small}

**Mode {mode_index} of {mode_count}** on {remote_name}

Give this mode a name and pick what should happen. Every field is a full Home Assistant action sequence - you can use conditions, delays and templates just like in an automation. Leave a field empty and nothing happens.

The wheel provides the variables `level` (1-255), `level_pct` (0-100), `level_254` (clamped for lamps), `previous_level`, `delta` and `direction`.

> [!NOTE]
> With the internal or hybrid mode source, the action you picked as the mode switch also advances the mode. You can still bind it here; both will happen.

options.description:
{image_row}

Give the remote a name and pick the housing colour - the colour is only used for the illustrations, so it is easier to tell several remotes apart.

A mode is one full set of actions. Switching modes turns the same wheel into a light dimmer, a volume knob or anything else.

user.description:
{image}

Zigbee2MQTT reports {device_count} remote(s) that look like a BILRESA. Pick the one you want to configure.

Not the one you are looking for? Tick the box below to type the address by hand.

invalid_sequence.description:
The action for {action} in mode {mode} could not be loaded and is being skipped. Everything else on this remote keeps working.

Error: {error}

Open the remote, go to the affected mode and fix the sequence.
